---
/**
 * The Counting House - parent case study
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import WhatsAppCTA from '../../../components/ui/WhatsAppCTA';
import PhoneLink from '../../../components/ui/PhoneLink.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../../../config/site';

const { phone, url } = siteConfig;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'The Counting House', url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house` },
];

const articleProps = {
  headline: 'The Counting House — Listed Period Timber Restoration, Pontefract',
  url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house`,
  datePublished: '2024-01-01',
};
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Counting House Timber Restoration"
  description="A listed period building in Pontefract: timber windows, doors, frames and structural matching, repaired sympathetically with original fabric retained."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleProps={articleProps}
>
  <article>
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="py-16 lg:py-24 bg-paper">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <p class="text-oak uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">Listed Period Building — Pontefract</p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-4xl lg:text-6xl text-ink mb-6">
          The Counting House
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-ink/70 max-w-2xl">
          A listed period building in Pontefract town centre, with original timber windows, doors, frames, and detailing repaired by Heritage Joiners using a repair-first approach across multiple elements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <section class="py-16 bg-bone">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-12">
          <div class="lg:col-span-2 space-y-6">
            <div>
              <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The building</h2>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The Counting House is a listed period building in Pontefract town centre, associated with the town's commercial history. The building carries Georgian and Victorian-era detailing across its windows, doors, frames, and structural timber — original joinery that forms part of its character and its listed status.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70">
                It is the kind of building where each element has to be assessed individually. Some timber was in excellent condition despite its age; some had been compromised by earlier interventions or by moisture entering through failed elements nearby. The repair scope was dictated by condition, not by a blanket decision to renew.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The brief</h2>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The owner approached Heritage Joiners with timber decay across multiple windows, door frames, and associated elements. Earlier interventions had partially addressed some problems but had not resolved the underlying moisture paths. The brief was straightforward: assess each element honestly, recommend repair where viable and replacement where not, and deliver a programme of works that preserved as much original timber as possible.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70">
                No arbitrary targets were set for retention. The assessment drove the scope.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The work</h2>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The initial site visit covered every opening — each sash window, each door, each frame. Timber was probed and moisture-read to establish the extent of sound material. A condition summary was produced before any work began, with a recommendation for each element: retain and maintain, splice repair, partial section replacement, or full replacement.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The sash windows were the largest element of the work. Cords were replaced across several windows, where years of operation had worn them to failure. Cills and bottom rails where decay was confined to localised sections were splice-repaired — the sound parent timber retained and new profile-matched timber scarfed in. Draught-proofing brush seals were fitted into routed rebates where window operation allowed it. The result is windows that operate correctly and do not leak air, without any alteration to their appearance from outside.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                Door and frame work addressed rotten lower rails on external doors — a common failure point where rain sits in the threshold zone. The lower sections were cut back to sound timber, new material fitted to match the original profile, and the threshold detailed to shed water rather than hold it. Ironmongery was checked, adjusted, and replaced where necessary.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70">
                Across all the repair work, the final stage was matching: colour, sheen, grain direction. New timber spliced into old timber stands out immediately unless this stage is done carefully. Test boards on the actual material, adjusted stains and oils until the match was acceptable in natural light, then applied before the work was signed off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Timber matching</h2>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The finishing work on a listed building is what distinguishes a conservation-minded repair from a standard joinery job. New timber machined to the original profile looks right in section; it does not look right in colour or texture until work is done to close the gap.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70">
                At The Counting House, the approach was consistent across all repair sections: assess the existing finish system, run test boards to match colour and sheen in natural light, treat the surface of the new timber to reduce visual contrast, and apply the matched system before any paint overcoat. The detail is documented in the <a href="/case-studies/counting-house/timber-matching" class="text-oak underline">timber matching case study page</a>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Outcome</h2>
              <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
                The majority of original timber elements across the building were retained. Where new sections were spliced in, they were profile-matched and finish-blended to sit naturally against the surrounding fabric. The listed character of the building was preserved throughout — no element was replaced that could be soundly repaired.
              </p>
              <p class="text-ink/70">
                The building now requires only routine maintenance on its timber elements. The moisture paths that caused the earlier decay have been addressed, which is the necessary condition for any repair to last.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="p-6 bg-paper rounded-lg border border-ink/10">
              <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-ink mb-4">Project overview</h3>
              <dl class="space-y-3 text-sm">
                <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-ink/10 pb-2">
                  <dt class="text-ink/60">Location</dt>
                  <dd class="text-ink">Pontefract, West Yorkshire</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-ink/10 pb-2">
                  <dt class="text-ink/60">Type</dt>
                  <dd class="text-ink">Listed Period Building</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-ink/10 pb-2">
                  <dt class="text-ink/60">Services</dt>
                  <dd class="text-ink text-right">Sash windows, doors, frames, rot &amp; splice, timber matching</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex justify-between">
                  <dt class="text-ink/60">Outcome</dt>
                  <dd class="text-ink text-right">Majority of original timber retained</dd>
                </div>
              </dl>
            </div>

            <div class="p-6 bg-paper rounded-lg border border-ink/10">
              <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-ink mb-3">Related service pages</h3>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
                <li><a href="/services/sash-window-repair" class="text-oak hover:underline">Sash Window Repair</a></li>
                <li><a href="/services/doors-and-frames" class="text-oak hover:underline">Doors &amp; Frames</a></li>
                <li><a href="/services/rot-and-splice" class="text-oak hover:underline">Rot &amp; Splice Repair</a></li>
                <li><a href="/services/timber-colour-matching-and-finishing" class="text-oak hover:underline">Colour Matching &amp; Finishing</a></li>
                <li><a href="/services/listed-and-period-property-repairs" class="text-oak hover:underline">Listed &amp; Period Property Repairs</a></li>
                <li><a href="/areas/pontefract" class="text-oak hover:underline">Pontefract area page</a></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Sub-pages -->
    <section class="py-16 bg-paper">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-ink mb-8">This project in detail</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <a href="/case-studies/counting-house/sash-windows" class="block p-6 bg-bone rounded-lg border border-ink/10 hover:border-oak transition-colors">
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-ink mb-2">Sash Window Restoration</h3>
            <p class="text-ink/70 text-sm">Cord replacement, splice repairs to cills and rails, draught-proofing, and easing across multiple sash windows.</p>
            <span class="inline-block mt-4 text-oak font-display text-sm">Read more &rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/case-studies/counting-house/timber-doors" class="block p-6 bg-bone rounded-lg border border-ink/10 hover:border-oak transition-colors">
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-ink mb-2">Timber Door Restoration</h3>
            <p class="text-ink/70 text-sm">Lower rail rot, threshold work, ironmongery, and frame repairs on external period doors.</p>
            <span class="inline-block mt-4 text-oak font-display text-sm">Read more &rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/case-studies/counting-house/timber-matching" class="block p-6 bg-bone rounded-lg border border-ink/10 hover:border-oak transition-colors">
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-ink mb-2">Timber Matching &amp; Finishing</h3>
            <p class="text-ink/70 text-sm">Profile matching, colour blending, and period-accurate finishing across all repaired timber elements.</p>
            <span class="inline-block mt-4 text-oak font-display text-sm">Read more &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="py-16 bg-ink text-bone">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
        <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">Need a similar project?</h2>
        <p class="text-bone/70 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Send photos of the timber in question — windows, doors, structural elements — and we will give you an honest first view on repair viability.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <WhatsAppCTA client:load />
          <PhoneLink phone={phone.tel} display={phone.display} variant="secondary" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</CaseStudyLayout>
